description:
FILED s440 (Fable), FOUND BY THE SHAPES CORPUS on its first run (Pl/t/shapes/sub-heads.pl packages each lifted snippet): Pl/t/sig-param-shadow-01.t's rows pass one per program, the same snippets side by side in block-scoped packages do not.

REPRODUCERS (perl 5.40: f(5) / g(5) in every row):

    { package S01; use feature "signatures"; sub f ($x) { "f($x)" } my $x = f 5; print "$x\n"; }
    { package S02; use feature "signatures"; sub f ($x) { "g($x)" } my $x = f 5; print "$x\n"; }
        PCL: "statement dropped at line 2: my $x = f 5; -- Bug. Fell through. Missing case: [" then f(5), then a runtime crash

    package S01; use feature "signatures"; sub f ($x) { "f($x)" } my $x = f 5; print "$x\n";
    package S02; use feature "signatures"; sub f ($x = 1) { "g($x)" } my $y = f 5; print "$y\n";
        PCL: the same (statement-form packages, so it is not the block)

    { package S01; use feature "signatures"; sub f ($x) { "f($x)" } print f(5), "\n"; }
    { package S02; use feature "signatures"; sub f ($x = 1) { "g($x)" } print f(5), "\n"; }
        PCL: f(5) then DIES "Too many arguments for subroutine 'S02::f' (got 1; expected 0)"

    { package S01; sub f { "f($_[0])" } my $x = f 5; ... }  { package S02; sub f { "g($_[0])" } my $x = f 5; ... }
        PCL: RIGHT (no signatures) -- so it is the SIGNATURE machinery, not sub declaration

THE EMISSION SAYS WHERE: S02's sub comes out as `;; sub f ($S02::x = 1) { ... }` / `(p-check-arity "S02::f" (length @_) 0 0 nil nil)` / body reads `S02::$x` -- the second package's signature PARAMETER was not recognised as a parameter and was qualified as the package global.  The first package's f is right.  So something keyed by the bare sub name ('f') -- the signature-param canon list (#454's _signature_param_canons), the declared-sub arity record, or the call-site classification (#453's is_named_unary / the spec table) -- is looked up across packages and the second declaration loses.  This is #421's family ("prototype table keyed by BARE name collides across packages", fixed for PROTOTYPES in s427); signatures evidently have a second copy of the key (CLAUDE.md rule 11 -- find the sibling, one key).  The `f 5` drop is downstream of the wrong arity (0 params -> the operand site has no case).

BAR: the four rows above vs perl; block-scoped AND statement-scoped packages; the same name in main + one package; three packages; a signature with defaults and a slurpy; `f(5)` and `f 5`; the inverse (one package, two subs; one sub, two packages WITHOUT signatures) byte-identical; corpus-diff; gate-SET scan if a classifier widens; guard rows in Pl/t/sig-param-shadow-01.t.  Q7 filler (plan-post-s433); the shapes file keeps the shape, so the A/B will show this fix as a DIFF in Pl/t/shapes/sub-heads.pl -- that is the corpus working.


FIXED s440d (Fable).  NOT a name-keyed registry after all -- the NAME only decided whether a spanning file lexical or a second block-package region existed.  TWO rewriters were offered the signature PARAMETER's own Symbol (PPI gives `($x = 1)` as a Structure::Signature when the feature is in force from an earlier line) and neither knew it was a declaration: (a) `_requalify_region` asked `_binding_at` (a scope walk; the signature is a sibling of the body, not its ancestor) and rewrote it to `$Pkg::x`; (b) the file-lexical span renamer asked `_ref_shadowed` (same blind spot) when a `my $x` in an earlier package spans into the sub's section.  Either way the head read `sub f ($S02::x = 1)`, arity 0/0, body reading the global, so `f 5` dropped and `f(5)` died.  ONE predicate `Pl::Parser2::_symbol_is_signature_param` (the leading Symbol of a top-level part of a Structure::Signature; a Symbol after `=` is a use) now answers both resolvers first.  Guards: Pl/t/sig-param-shadow-01.t +4 rows (three positives fail on a HEAD worktree, the inverse passes on both); corpus-diff: the 111 differ only in 3 files' drop-reason TEXT (the s440 `_dd` now renders tokens compactly on one line -- `['Token::Symbol<$obj>', ...]`; census counts unchanged), shapes: sub-heads.pl fixed as predicted; lib A/B 22 SAME; generation v2-183.
